@page "/getapp"
@layout PublicLayout
@rendermode @(new InteractiveServerRenderMode(prerender: false))
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@using QMgr.Application.DTOs
@using QMgr.Web.Components.Shared
@using QMgr.Web.Services
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@inject IMobileAppApiService MobileApi
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@*
    HOW A PERSON ACTUALLY OBTAINS THE APP.

    On the ERP the endpoints existed for a full day before anybody noticed there was no way for a
    human to reach them — no link, no page, nothing in the UI. A user would have had to be told a URL
    by hand. So this page exists, and it is linked from the SIGN-IN SCREEN, because the people who
    need the app are exactly the people who cannot get in yet.

    ANONYMOUS, for the same reason: somebody setting up a new front desk has not signed in, and often
    cannot until they have this.

    Four rules this page keeps, each of which is a way the ERP's version went wrong first:
      * Only downloads whose FILE exists are rendered. A page is where an operator's mistake becomes
        a customer's problem; never render a button that 404s.
      * Earlier builds are COLLAPSED, and the warning comes BEFORE the links — Android will not
        install an older version over a newer one, so the app must be uninstalled first, which erases
        everything on the device. That consequence is why the section is quiet.
      * An empty state is EXPLAINED. A blank panel reads as broken.
      * "No builds published on this SERVER", never "for this workspace" — one app serves every
        school, and the other wording sends an administrator hunting for a per-tenant upload that
        does not exist.
*@

<QPageTitle Text="Get mobile app" />

@*
    THE SAME SHELL AS THE SIGN-IN PAGE, deliberately.

    This page shipped with a card of its own and was wrong twice for it: unbranded (a school on its
    own domain met the vendor's name one click from its own crest — the exact complaint the join page
    was fixed for on 2026-09-21) and unreadable, because .getapp__card painted a light surface while
    PublicLayout's ground is dark and the heading inherited the dark ink.

    Reusing login-container / login-card / QBrandMark fixes both at once and cannot drift from them
    later, which a second set of card styles certainly would.
*@
<div class="login-container" style="@Host.BrandingStyle">
    <AmbientBackground />
    <div class="login-card wide getapp">
        <div class="login-header">
            <QBrandMark Heading="Get mobile app" />
        </div>

    @* ── The workspace address ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       First, and with a copy button, because a mistyped workspace is the commonest first-run
       failure and it surfaces much later as a login the person cannot explain. Handing it to them
       beats asking them to remember it. *@
    <section class="getapp__card">
        <h2>
            1. The address to type
            <QInfo Label="The address to type" Text="@AddressHelp" />
        </h2>
        <p>When the app asks for your workspace, enter this:</p>
        <div class="getapp__workspace">
            <code id="workspace-address">@WorkspaceAddress</code>
            <QButton Variant="secondary" Size="sm" OnClick="CopyWorkspaceAsync">
                <QIcon Name="clipboard" Size="14" /> @(copied ? "Copied" : "Copy")
            </QButton>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ── Downloads ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
    <section class="getapp__card">
        <h2>
            2. Download it
            <QInfo Label="Download it" Text="Android asks you to allow installs from this site the first time. The app needs Android 7 or newer on a 64-bit device — the build is 64-bit only, which halves the download." />
        </h2>

        @if (loading)
        {
            <p class="getapp__note">Looking for the latest build&hellip;</p>
        }
        else if (loadFailed)
        {
            @* Distinct from "nothing published". The reader can retry this one; they cannot retry
               the other. Telling them apart is the whole reason GetReleasesAsync returns null
               rather than an empty list on a failure. *@
            <QEmptyState Icon="cloud-slash"
                         Title="Downloads unavailable"
                         Message="We could not reach the download service just now. Please try again in a few minutes." />
        }
        else if (latest is null)
        {
            <QEmptyState Icon="box-seam"
                         Title="No builds yet"
                         Message="No app build has been published on this server yet. Ask your administrator." />
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="getapp__latest">
                <div class="getapp__latest-meta">
                    <span class="getapp__version">Version @latest.Version</span>
                    <span class="getapp__size">@Megabytes(latest.SizeBytes)</span>
                </div>
                @* A plain anchor, not QButton: this is a NAVIGATION to a file on another origin, and QButton
                   renders a <button> with no href. Styled to match so it does not read as a stray link. *@
                @* The download never leaves the page — the browser saves the file and stays here — so
                   without this the only sign a press did anything was the button's pressed colour.
                   The spinner is QButton's own. A second press while it spins is swallowed
                   (preventDefault), or two copies of a 13 MB file start at once. *@
                <a class="q-btn q-btn--primary getapp__dl @(downloadStarting ? "q-btn--disabled" : "")" href="@latest.Url"
                   aria-busy="@(downloadStarting ? "true" : "false")"
                   @onclick="StartDownloadAsync" @onclick:preventDefault="downloadStarting">
                    @if (downloadStarting)
                    {
                        <span class="q-btn__spinner"></span> <span>Downloading&hellip;</span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <QIcon Name="download" Size="16" /> <span>Download for Android</span>
                    }
                </a>
            </div>
            @if (downloadStarted)
            {
                <p class="getapp__note" role="status">Download started. See your browser's downloads.</p>
            }

            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(latest.Notes))
            {
                <p class="getapp__note">@latest.Notes</p>
            }

            @if (earlier.Count > 0)
            {
                <details class="getapp__earlier">
                    <summary>Earlier versions (@earlier.Count)</summary>
                    @* THE WARNING COMES FIRST. Not styling — it is the reason this section is
                       collapsed at all. *@
                    <p class="getapp__warn">
                        <QIcon Name="exclamation-triangle" Size="14" />
                        Android will not install an older version over a newer one. The app has to be
                        uninstalled first, which erases everything stored on the device. Only do this
                        if you have been asked to.
                    </p>
                    <ul class="getapp__earlier-list">
                        @foreach (var release in earlier)
                        {
                            <li>
                                <span>Version @release.Version</span>
                                @if (release.Available)
                                {
                                    <a href="@release.Url">Download</a>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    @* Reported, not linked. The manifest is history; the disk is what
                                       can be installed. *@
                                    <span class="getapp__gone">No longer on this server</span>
                                }
                            </li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                </details>
            }
        }
    </section>

    @* ── QR to this page ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       So the address is carried to a handset by scanning rather than dictated across a room.
       Uses qrcodejs, which App.razor already loads for visitor badges — no new dependency.

       The heading says "Open this on your phone", not "scan to set it up": the scan opens THIS
       PAGE on the phone, which is what it can honestly promise before the app exists. See the
       comment on scanUrl for why it is no longer a qmgr:// link. *@
    <section class="getapp__card">
        <h2>3. Scan on your phone</h2>
        <div id="getapp-qr" class="getapp__qr"></div>
    </section>

        @* A BUTTON, not a link: signing in is the act this page leads to, and every other action
           here (Copy, Download) is already a button. "Sign In" rather than "Back to sign in": a
           button label names its destination, and "back" is the browser's word, not ours.

           A styled ANCHOR, not QButton, for the reason stated on the download button above — QButton
           renders a <button> and has no Href, so a navigation has to be a link wearing the button
           class. Passing Href to it would render an inert button and component-param-check fails it. *@
        <div class="getapp__foot">
            <a class="q-btn q-btn--secondary" href="/login"><QIcon Name="box-arrow-in-right" Size="16" /> Sign In</a>
            <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>
        </div>
        @* The copyright, not the attribution line — see Login.razor. QCopyright reads the host itself. *@
        <QCopyright Class="login-copyright" />
    </div>
</div>

<style>
    /* Everything here is scoped under .login-container, like the rest of auth-pages.css and for the
       same reason: these class names are generic enough to collide with the dashboard's own. */
    .login-container .getapp { text-align: left; }

    .login-container .getapp__card {
        background: var(--qm-bg-elevated);
        border: 1px solid var(--qm-border);
        border-radius: var(--qm-radius-lg);
        padding: 16px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
    }
    .login-container .getapp__card h2 { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: var(--qm-text-md); color: var(--qm-text-primary); }
    .login-container .getapp__card p { margin: 0 0 10px; font-size: var(--qm-text-base); color: var(--qm-text-secondary); }

    .login-container .getapp__workspace { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .login-container .getapp__workspace code {
        font-size: var(--qm-text-md);
        padding: 7px 11px;
        border-radius: var(--qm-radius-md);
        background: var(--qm-bg-card);
        color: var(--qm-text-primary);
        word-break: break-all;
    }

    .login-container .getapp__note { font-size: var(--qm-text-sm); color: var(--qm-text-secondary); }

    .login-container .getapp__latest { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .login-container .getapp__latest-meta { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
    .login-container .getapp__version { font-weight: 600; color: var(--qm-text-primary); }
    .login-container .getapp__size { font-size: var(--qm-text-sm); color: var(--qm-text-secondary); }

    .login-container .getapp__earlier { margin-top: 14px; border-top: 1px solid var(--qm-border); padding-top: 12px; }
    .login-container .getapp__earlier summary { cursor: pointer; font-size: var(--qm-text-sm); color: var(--qm-text-secondary); }
    .login-container .getapp__warn {
        display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 7px;
        margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px 12px;
        border-radius: var(--qm-radius-md);
        background: rgba(var(--qm-warning-rgb), 0.12);
        color: var(--qm-text-primary);
        font-size: var(--qm-text-sm);
    }
    .login-container .getapp__earlier-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
    .login-container .getapp__earlier-list li {
        display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
        gap: 10px; padding: 7px 0; font-size: var(--qm-text-sm);
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--qm-border);
    }
    .login-container .getapp__earlier-list li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
    .login-container .getapp__gone { color: var(--qm-text-muted); }

    /* A QR is READ BY A CAMERA, so its quiet zone must be white whatever the theme — this is the
       one literal on the page and it is a literal on purpose. */
    .login-container .getapp__qr {
        display: flex; justify-content: center; padding: 12px;
        background: #ffffff; border-radius: var(--qm-radius-md);
    }

    .login-container .getapp__foot {
        display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 10px;
        flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 18px;
        font-size: var(--qm-text-sm); color: var(--qm-text-secondary);
    }
</style>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Which host the browser arrived on, cascaded by <c>App.razor</c> — the only file that can see
    /// <c>Request.Host</c>, because it is server-rendered on every request whatever the render mode.
    /// </summary>
    [CascadingParameter] public TenantHostContext Host { get; set; } = TenantHostContext.Platform;

    /// <summary>
    /// Standing explanation for the address, in the QInfo beside the heading rather than as prose
    /// under it. Both branches were a paragraph on the page until 2026-09-22; they are read once.
    /// </summary>
    private string AddressHelp => Host.IsTenantHost
        ? "This is your school's own address, so the app shows your name and colours before anyone signs in."
        : "Sign in with your email address or your staff username. Many staff have no email address on file, and a username works exactly the same way.";

    private bool loading = true;

    /// <summary>True for the few seconds after a press — long enough for the browser's own download
    /// indicator to appear. A web page cannot see a download's progress, so it does not pretend to.</summary>
    private bool downloadStarting;
    private bool downloadStarted;

    private async Task StartDownloadAsync()
    {
        if (downloadStarting) return;
        downloadStarting = true;
        StateHasChanged();
        await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(4));
        downloadStarting = false;
        downloadStarted = true;
    }
    private bool loadFailed;
    private bool copied;

    private AppReleaseDto? latest;
    private List<AppReleaseDto> earlier = new();

    /// <summary>
    /// What the person types into the app.
    ///
    /// <para>On a school's own domain it is that domain, and the app then wears their name and
    /// colours from the sign-in screen onwards. On the shared platform host it is simply this host,
    /// because every school that has not bought a domain reaches Q-Mgr there and which school is
    /// decided by whoever signs in — not by the address.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Read from the BROWSER's own address rather than from configuration, so the page always
    /// prints the address that actually reached it. A configured value would be one more thing to get
    /// wrong per deployment, and getting it wrong here means a workspace nobody can connect to.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string WorkspaceAddress =>
        !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Host.Host)
            ? Host.Host
            : new Uri(Navigation.BaseUri).Host;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var releases = await MobileApi.GetReleasesAsync("android");

        if (releases is null)
        {
            loadFailed = true;
        }
        else
        {
            latest = releases.Latest;
            // Everything that is not the newest installable build. Kept even when its artefact has
            // been pruned, so the page can say "no longer on this server" rather than link to a 404.
            earlier = releases.Releases
                .Where(r => latest is null || r.VersionCode != latest.VersionCode)
                .ToList();
        }

        loading = false;
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender) return;

        // THE QR ENCODES AN https URL — THIS PAGE — NOT A PRIVATE SCHEME.
        //
        // It used to encode qmgr://workspace?host=… , which decoded perfectly (verified with jsQR)
        // and was useless to every single person who scanned it:
        //
        //   * A custom scheme resolves only when the app that registered it is ALREADY INSTALLED.
        //     Everyone reading this page is here BECAUSE THEY DO NOT HAVE THE APP. The scan had
        //     nothing to open and failed silently, or raised "no app can open this link".
        //   * Both platforms' CAMERA apps refuse non-http(s) schemes outright as an anti-abuse
        //     measure, so even an installed app frequently would not have been offered.
        //
        // An https URL degrades the right way round: a phone with no app opens this page and can
        // download it, and once the app registers this address as an Android App Link / iOS
        // Universal Link the same scan deep-links straight into provisioning. That is the standard
        // pattern precisely because it works in both states.
        //
        // The host comes from the browser's own address for the reason on WorkspaceAddress, so a
        // white-labelled tenant's QR points at the tenant's own domain rather than ours.
        var scanUrl = $"https://{WorkspaceAddress}/getapp";

        try
        {
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("visitorBadge.renderQr", "getapp-qr", scanUrl);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // The QR is a convenience; the address above it is the real instruction. A failed render
            // must not take the page down, and there is nothing useful to say about it.
        }
    }

    private async Task CopyWorkspaceAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("navigator.clipboard.writeText", WorkspaceAddress);
            copied = true;
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Clipboard access can be refused outright. The address is on screen and selectable, so
            // there is nothing to recover — the label simply does not change to "Copied", which is
            // honest.
        }
    }

    private static string Megabytes(long bytes)
        => bytes <= 0 ? "" : $"{bytes / 1024d / 1024d:0.#} MB";
}
